# Instructions

Audit only the existing decision at checks.{{ key }}: continuing the current block from the preceding main text as one natural paragraph. The complete three-page evidence is in local_raw_evidence.

First locate the genuine main-text predecessor in preceding_source_index using preceding_source_candidates, then audit the current incoming edge. ownership_hint and immediate_paragraph_hint are not facts: OCR or the algorithm may have misclassified a footnote as paragraph. Do not simply compare the last array entry with the current block.

If the nearest entry is a note, page number or running header, skip it and check the earlier genuine main-text candidates. Judge only the incoming edge of the current page; an independent ownership error on a neighboring page does not by itself invalidate this question. Do not use anything outside the three supplied pages or select next_page as an incoming predecessor.

The genuine predecessor must be the nearest main text after excluding ancillary material. Do not skip intervening genuine main text to choose an older passage whose wording happens to fit. Candidates are not a pool of interchangeable text. If the nearest main text is already complete, the current block may start a new paragraph even when both describe the same event.

Use only the supplied local raw text to decide whether the two blocks form one natural paragraph. Do not audit the whole page or the fields of neighboring pages.

A split word or unfinished grammatical construction requires continuation. Clearly independent utterances, headings and numbered items require separate paragraphs. Do not reverse these rules: lack of a mandatory dependency does not prove that an existing join is wrong. Shared topic, question-and-answer wording or a period alone does not decide the boundary.

Do not proofread text, supply missing OCR characters or assume distant content. Even when the current block is long, inspect its opening and the direct seam with the preceding ending.

This question audits a JOIN, not a proposed break. The two decisions have different failure conditions: an existing break is wrong when it cuts an obligatory dependency; an existing join is wrong when it combines clearly independent reading units. Do not reject an existing join merely because a break would also be acceptable. A conjunction, consequence, contrast or shared topic alone proves neither decision wrong.

A paragraph introducing a quotation and the following independent quotation may be separate paragraphs. An attribution or source after a quotation may also stand alone. A colon or an attribution relationship is not evidence that joining is mandatory.

Judge only whether the existing incoming decision has a definite structural error. Do not infer unprovided first-line indentation from the overall box or impose your own paragraph-layout preference.

The current decision is a JOIN. First combine one or two characters at the predecessor ending and target opening to check for a complete word. If the two parts of a word are split across pages, keeping this join is correct; do not be distracted by other complete sentences later in the current block.

# True

The current block can legitimately continue the preceding block as one natural paragraph; no definite structural error in this join is established.

# False

The current block must start a new natural paragraph; the existing join incorrectly combines two independent paragraphs.
